<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Setthathirath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1534">1534</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Setthathirath I, Xaysettha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Setthathirath was king of Lan Xang from 1548 to 1571 and briefly also king of Lan Na from 1546. In 1560 he moved the Lao capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> to Vientiane, where he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pha-that-luang">Pha That Luang</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haw-phra-kaew">Haw Phra Kaew</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, which he had brought from Chiang Mai. He resisted Burmese invasions under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> and disappeared during a campaign in the south in 1571.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/setthathirath.jpg" alt="Setthathirath" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alcyon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setthathirat.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Setthathirath was born on 24 January 1534, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photisarath">Photisarath</a> of Lan Xang and Princess Yotkhamtip, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ket-klao">Ketklao</a> of Lan Na. When Ketklao died without a male heir, the high officials and Buddhist monks of Lan Na agreed in 1546 to give the throne to the young prince, known there as Chao Upayo. He took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> as his personal palladium. On his father's death his half-brothers Prince Tha Heua and Prince Lanchang began to divide Lan Xang, and he hurried home with the sacred images, leaving Chiang Mai under Queen Chiraprapha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He subdued his rivals and pardoned Lanchang, but the nobles of Lan Na, feeling he had stayed away too long, chose the Shan prince Mekuti in 1551, and his attempts to retake the kingdom in 1553 and 1555 won back only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a>. In 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> took Chiang Mai. Setthathirath strengthened Vientiane's defences, built a great palace and renovated That Luang before formally making it the capital in 1560, a role it kept for some two hundred and fifty years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-xieng-thong">Wat Xieng Thong</a> rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>.</p><p>An alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, to be sealed by his marriage to Princess Thepkasattri, failed when Maha Thammaracha seized her on the way to Lan Xang in 1564. When the Burmese took Vientiane, he harassed them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> raids until disease and hunger drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> back in 1565, leaving Lan Xang the only independent Tai kingdom. He joined an Ayutthayan rising in 1567, killing five pursuing Burmese generals in an ambush, but in 1569 was trapped near Phetchabun. After a second Burmese occupation of Vientiane, his counterattack in 1570 took more than 30,000 prisoners, 100 elephants and 2,300 pieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. He then campaigned against the Longvek Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His heir, Noi Hno Muang Keo Koumane, was a toddler, and the boy's maternal grandfather Sen Soulintha declared himself king. Years of unsteady rule followed until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> conquered the country in 1574 and took the child to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. The prince returned to be crowned in Vientiane in 1591. Setthathirath is counted among the great leaders of Lao history, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-xieng-thong">Wat Xieng Thong</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haw-phra-kaew">Haw Phra Kaew</a>, Wat Ong Teu Mahawihan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pha-that-luang">Pha That Luang</a> remain among his works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Setthathirath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1545 King Ket Klao was killed, and the nobles invited the young prince <b>Setthathirath of Lan Xang</b>, whose mother was a Chiang Mai princess, to take the throne; he left in 1547 to claim the Lao throne, and the Emerald Buddha was taken to Lan Xang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Toungoo king Bayinnaung conquered Lan Na and Ayutthaya, <b>Setthathirath</b> (r. 1548–1571) allied with Ayutthaya, an alliance commemorated in the stupa of Phra That Si Song Rak, and moved his capital to Vientiane, further from the Burmese advance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in Attapeu Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1558" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Setthathirath"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1558">What was here in 1558? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
